WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.689
Film Courage: Can you tell us about an actual
assignment you've received and go

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:07.020
through the timeline and how you worked
your way through the deadline? Maybe you

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:11.309
came in over it? Maybe under it?
Gary Goldstein, Screenwriter/Playwright: Yeah well you know it with assignments.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:16.800
It's often there's a
particular deadline or a goal

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.740
that you're trying to reach but then
then the goal posts keep changing just

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.300
because schedules changed and and you
know you end up with more time sometimes

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.920
and it was a little less time you know
I've worked on films that that they were

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.669
they were they realize they needed to
shoot a little sooner than they thought

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:38.670
so you have to step it up but generally
there's enough time to to do your draft

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.290
to do several other drafts you can
i-i've on a lot of my TV movies I've

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.770
worked throughout you know throughout
the production even you know where where

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.100
if something has been a problem I've
changed something the director will call

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.780
and I'll change something or or alter
something or add a scene or that kind of

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.680
thing so sometimes it goes even you know
beyond the the just turning in the

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.969
script stage but you know generally the
way it goes is that you know you get the

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:07.650
outline approved you I I structure the
script I outline the script you know do

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.189
that step process that I do and then I
start writing and I do generally

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:16.080
schedule myself to you know take out
like a calendar and say you know okay

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.100
I've got six weeks to write this first
draft so I'm gonna but you know by this

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.400
week I want to get here I want to finish
it here I want to have two weeks to

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.060
rewrite that kind of thing and that
helps me I used to find writing anything

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.189
down and seeing anything visually really
helps me you know make it happen and

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:36.979
then I just try to just barrel forward
you know and and and and also if I see

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:40.650
honestly if I see a you know a glitch or
I see something that's not working to

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.490
make sure to check in with my executive
or my producer and say look what do you

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.700
think about this you know I'm not I
don't worry about like not having the

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.579
answer or something like that I want to
make sure that you know that that were

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:54.149
all in agreement on something and I'm a
big believer in collaboration I like

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:57.540
collaborating especially when there are
good ideas and you know and and

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.369
sometimes you just talk out a problem
and and just talking it out you you

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:05.399
can't it kind of opens up what the
problem is or the person you're talking

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:08.819
to has an idea well why don't you try
this that might work better and you know

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:11.430
and that's a big help and that can take
a little time you know that can take a

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.760
little time you have to
backtrack and then you know catch up

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:22.189
again but but by and large you know what
happens is where you where you end up

00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:25.790
losing time is in the rewriting you know
we're not losing time as much as like

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.310
what the schedule can kind of kind of go
off kilter

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.579
you know because you may you may get
something in on time or even a little

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:34.700
bit earlier there are times I'll get
something in a little earlier than

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.909
expected
other times generally I think I hit the

00:02:37.909 --> 00:02:42.500
deadline sometimes Oh maybe a little bit
late but but but then then it takes time

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.489
for everybody to read the script and and
for to get input from maybe the director

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:48.650
if the director is on board and then
before you know it you're writing

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.010
another draft and another draft I mean
sometimes it could be several drafts

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.700
sometimes as many drafts it just depends
upon you know how many voices there are

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.209
and and and you know certain production
issues that they know are gonna happen

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:03.650
they they ready to shoot the script and
then their location scouting and they

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.239
realize they don't have a church but
they have a you know a bowling alley

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:11.720
whatever you know and so you have to
rearrange the scene for the location

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.470
that's available whether it can be an
issue sometimes you have to change there

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.829
lots of changes along the way one thing
is I'm gonna go into production that's

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:21.560
the you know that's the good news
that's the high-class problem because

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.540
you're making changes because the movies
actually being made but you know I I I

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.459
like to I like to work in waves you know
I like to work in movements you know

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:39.079
where where where I can I like what's
the word stages of closure so I like to

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:43.250
be able to finish a draft and then start
over and rewrite that draft and you know

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:48.680
so that it goes in more quicker stages
than one massively long stage you know

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.220
where you're just you know just
agonizing over something for months and

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.870
months and months I'd rather write it
faster hopefully it's good and then go

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.010
back and still clean sweep through it
and fix everything and just keep them

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.939
again and go back to the clay keep
molding the clay to me it's it's very

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:09.650
motivating to have new drafts all the
time you know and and to see to see that

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.620
you know you see everything sort of like
it's almost a graphic relief it sort of

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.489
comes out a little bit more at you and
you see what's happening but again

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:20.840
everybody's process is different you
know I over the course of time I become

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.050
very much not in agonizer writer
an agonising writer I I I just you know

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:31.560
just pushed forward you know head down
push forward and and just really try to

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.860
try to do the best I can like everybody
does but you know I just worry less you

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.760
know I just try to do a great job
hopefully and and you know and just I

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.270
know I can fix things you know I always
feel like I have enough tools I've

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.210
written so many things that I do
understand how to how to fix things and

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:49.840
had to come up with ideas if somebody
doesn't like the product how I can fix

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.320
and how we can make it better
what why it work what was my intention

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:58.590
that kind of thing so so yeah

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.419
you

